560: ## P3K.7. Proved: the real trace computes the actual spin return index
561: 
562: Cap \(X\) along \(F\times S^1\) with \(C=D^3\times S^1\), using
563: the fixed normal framing chosen for the actual conifold replacement.
564: Denote the resulting closed four-manifold by \(\bar X\).  Its cap
565: relative to \(V\) is
566: 
567: \[
568:  H_a=(W\cup_FD^3)\times S^1
569:       =D^2_a\times S^1_b\times S^1_\gamma.
570: \]
571: 
572: The subscripts retain the marked boundary cycles.  This cap kills
573: \(a\).  The usual elliptic cap kills \(\gamma\); their boundary
574: markings are not silently equated.  Both have signature zero.
575: 
576: Delete the normal disc bundle of \(X\) from \(Z\), and glue
577: \((C\times S^2)\times I\) along the resulting boundary collar.
578: The resulting two closed boundary components are the physical
579: positive return family \(P_f\) and the normal sphere bundle
580: \(B=S(\bar\nu)\to\bar X\).  On the outer side this is precisely
581: the replacement of the fixed \(F\times D^3\) by \(D^3\times S^2\),
582: performed in every parameter fibre.  On the inner side it completes
583: the normal sphere bundle over the cap.  We now specify its two
584: boundary orientations and the return convention explicitly.
585: 
586: Let the circle-first quotient convention be
587: \(\Omega_{X_6}=d\delta\wedge\Omega_Y\).  In the fixed normal
588: complex two-plane the weights are \((-1,+1)\).  Conjugating the
589: negative-weight complex coordinate reverses its real orientation;
590: contracting the positive Hopf volume by the circle vector reverses
591: the outward cone convention once more.  The two signs give
592: \(\Omega_Y=\Omega_F\wedge\Omega_{\nu,\mathrm{Hopf}}\), where
593: the meridian sphere has the positive Hopf orientation.  The graph
594: part of \(X\) has the complex page orientation, and the oriented
595: compression has \(\partial X=F\times S^1_\theta\), in that order.
596: Give \(Z=Y\times D^2\) its product orientation.  Since \(\dim Y=5\),
597: the outward boundary orientation is
598: 
599: \[
600:  \Omega_{\partial Z}=-\Omega_Y\wedge d\theta.
601: \]
602: 
603: The inner boundary of the deleted normal disc bundle is oriented
604: \(-\Omega_{\bar X}\wedge\Omega_{S^2}\).  The product cap preserves
605: these signs.  Thus the actual oriented bordism has boundary
606: \(-P_f\sqcup(-B)\), with \(P_f\) oriented by the fibre followed
607: by positive parameter, and \(B\) by the complex page followed
608: by the positive meridian sphere.
609: 
610: Jin's mapping torus \(M_f\) identifies \((z,0)\sim(f(z),1)\).
611: The physical positive family of the return \(f\), which identifies
612: \((z,1)\sim(f(z),0)\), is therefore \(P_f=M_{f^{-1}}\).
613: Characteristic-number additivity under inversion, together with
614: the two boundary signs just computed, gives
615: 
616: \[
617:  (k,\ell,A)(P_f)=-(k,\ell,A)(B),\qquad
618:  (k,\ell,A)_{\mathrm{Jin}}(f)=(k,\ell,A)(B).
619: \]
620: 
621: This records both reversals.  The following integrations on \(B\)
622: therefore compute Jin's invariants of the specified physical
623: positive return \(f\), with the stated positive meridian class.
624: 
625: The Euler line extends over the bordism.  On
626: \(F\times S^1\times S^2\) its Chern class is the meridian generator,
627: by the positive-weight SU(2) trivialization of P3F.7.  It therefore
628: extends over \(D^3\times S^1\times S^2\).  An ambiguity in the
629: boundary bundle isomorphism is a map to \(S^1\); its homotopy class
630: lies in \(H^1(S^2\times S^1\times S^2)=\mathbf Z\), and the
631: restriction from \(H^1(D^3\times S^1\times S^2)\) is an isomorphism.
632: It extends as well.  The bordism is spin: \(Y\times D^2\) is spin,
633: and the two possible spin structures on the gluing boundary differ
634: only on the same \(S^1\) factor, where either extends over the cap.
635: This identifies the actual spin return symbol and Euler line with
636: the inner boundary symbol by spin bordism, with no analytic corner
637: or contraction of exceptional curves.
638: 
639: Write \(e_v\) for the vertical Euler class of \(S(\bar\nu)\), and
640: define \(b\in H^2(\bar X;\mathbf Z)\) by
641: 
642: \[
643:  e_v=2x-\pi^*b.
644: \]
645: 
646: Existence and uniqueness follow from the sphere-bundle Gysin
647: sequence, since both classes have fibre integral two.  Since the
648: normal bundle of the graph page is framed and its chosen section
649: has \(s^*e_v=0\), P3K.6 gives \(b|_V=2c_1(M)\).  On the product
650: cap \(C\), \(b=0\).  Its restriction on the intermediate product
651: compression is zero as well: \(H^2(W\times S^1)\) injects into the
652: cohomology of its two boundary components.  This is seen from
653: \(H_1(W)=\mathbf Z\), \(H_2(W)=\mathbf Z\), and the boundary
654: torus and sphere, whose products with the circle generate both
655: classes of \(H_2(W\times S^1)\).  On the two boundaries the relevant
656: restriction is zero.  Equivalently,
657: \(H^2(H_a)\to H^2(T^3)\) is injective, so the vanishing boundary
658: restriction of \(b\) gives its vanishing on this whole cap.
659: 
660: Extend \(M\) trivially across \(H_a\), with class \(m\).  The
661: Mayer–Vietoris difference \(b-2m\) belongs to
662: \(\delta H^1(T^3;\mathbf Z)\).  As in P3K.5, its square and its
663: product with \(m\) vanish.  Therefore
664: 
665: \[
666:  \langle b^2,[\bar X]\rangle=4m_{V,\mathrm{rel}}^2=-8,
667:  \qquad
668:  \sigma(\bar X)=\sigma(V)=-8,
669:  \qquad
670:  \langle p_1(T\bar X),[\bar X]\rangle=-24.
671: \]
672: 
673: These use the actual page invariants P3F.10.  No identification of
674: the two different marked caps is needed.
675: 
676: Finally \(T_{\mathrm{vert}}B\oplus\mathbf R\cong\pi^*\bar\nu\),
677: so \(e_v^2=\pi^*p_1(\bar\nu)\).  With \(\pi_*x=1\), this gives
678: 
679: \[
680:  \pi_*x^2=b,\qquad
681:  \pi_*x^3=\frac{p_1(\bar\nu)+3b^2}{4},\qquad
682:  p_1(TB)=\pi^*(p_1(T\bar X)+p_1(\bar\nu)).
683: \]
684: 
685: The actual spin return index is consequently
686: 
687: \[
688:  A(f)=\frac{4\int_Bx^3-\int_Bp_1(TB)x}{24}
689:      =\frac{3\int_{\bar X}b^2-\int_{\bar X}p_1(T\bar X)}{24}
690:      =\frac{3(-8)-(-24)}{24}=0.
691: \]
692: 
693: The normal Pontryagin class cancels.  Thus no unproved normal
694: framing or spin claim about the elliptic cap enters the result.
695: For the original parameter return, P3F.11 states that this covered
696: return has index six times the original integer.  Since that
697: integer is integral, the original index is also zero.  The actual
698: Hopf-lift gluing P3F.8 and the extending \(a\)-powers P3F.4 then
699: identify the clutched sphere as standard.  **End of proof.**
700: 
701: ## P3K.8. Proved: a second, analytic computation of the return index
702: 
703: The actual bordism of P3K.6–7 also gives an analytic computation of
704: \(A\) that does not evaluate the signature of the page, the square
705: of its line, or a Pontryagin number.  Keep the inner sphere bundle
